ne more consideration: the fake class needs `fromtimestamp` and `utcfromtimestamp` as classmethods, and instances need `strftime`. The real code calls:\n- `datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(epoch_ts)` \u2192 fake class method\n- `datetime.datetime.utcfromtimestamp(epoch_ts)` \u2192 fake class method\n- `now.strftime(...)` many formats\n- `utcnow.strftime(...)` two formats\n\nMy fake:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeDateTime:\n    epoch_strftime_result = '1500000000'\n\n    def __init__(self, ts, utc=False):\n        self._ts = ts\n        self._utc = utc\n\n    @classmethod\n    def fromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n        return cls(ts)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def utcfromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n        return cls(ts, utc=True)\n\n    def strftime(self, fmt):\n        if fmt == '%s':\n            return type(self).epoch_strftime_result\n        if self._utc:\n            return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(self._ts, datetime.timezone.utc).strftime(fmt)\n        return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(self._ts).strftime(fmt)\n```\n\nwhere `_REAL_DATETIME = datetime.datetime` captured at import time (before any patching). Since I patch the module's `datetime` name (not the datetime module's attribute), `datetime.datetime` inside my test module still refers to the real class. But to be safe against the global-name patch, capture real class at module import: `_REAL_DATETIME = datetime.datetime`. Since I'm patching `date_time_module.datetime` (the reference in the module under test), the real datetime module is untouched, so `datetime.datetime` in my test file is fine. But capturing anyway is harmless and robust.\n\nClass attribute `epoch_strftime_result` \u2014 I'll set it per test via the subclass or instance attribute. Since fromtimestamp creates instances, I can set class attribute before calling collect:\n\n```python\n_FakeDateTime.epoch_strftime_result = ''\n```\n\nBut careful: class-level mutable state across tests \u2014 I'll set it in each test (and it's my own class, defined at modul