\u2014 normal path, full dict assertions (dynamic expected values + exact epoch values + tz values from fake time).\n2. `test_collect_epoch_empty_string_fallback` \u2014 fake '%s' \u2192 '' (first call) then valid; asserts epoch fallback value and full dict still returned. Hmm or ValueError version...\n\nUgh, I keep going back and forth. Let me settle with a pragmatic combination:\n\n- Test 2: '%s' returns '' on every call \u2192 expect ValueError (realistic platform simulation; covers line 52). Comment explains.\n- Test 3: '%s' returns '%s' on every call \u2192 expect ValueError (covers the '%'-prefix condition; line 52 again).\n\nWait, but then line 52 is covered by two tests and the function never completes in those. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 hmm, one concern with asserting ValueError: is that \"correct\"? On a platform where strftime('%s') returns '', ansible's collect() indeed raises ValueError. So the test documents actual behavior. It's correct as a characterization test. But a reviewer might say \"you're testing a crash \u2014 the fallback code at 52 exists precisely for this case, so the intended behavior is fallback, and the crash at 54 is a bug\". Testing the crash is still valid (it IS the current behavior), but testing intended behavior via per-call fake exercises lines 52 AND completes. \n\nYou know what, let me do BOTH styles but keep it tight:\n\n- Test 2 (empty %s, per-call): fake returns '' for the first '%s' call (epoch), then '1500000000' for subsequent calls (epoch_int). Assert epoch == '1500000000' (fallback used), epoch_int == '1500000000', and full dict present. This covers line 52 and the rest of the function. Comment: simulates a platform where %s is unsupported for the first lookup; the per-call distinction is required because line 54 would otherwise raise ValueError (int('')).\n\nHmm, that comment is awkward. Alternatively:\n\n- Test 2 (empty %s always): assert ValueError with match \"invalid literal\". Covers line 52. Comment: on