r-call it is for tests 2-3. Actually, let me simplify: one fake class parameterized by a list of '%s' results consumed in order:\n\n```python\ndef _make_fake_datetime_class(epoch_strftime_results):\n    \"\"\"Build a fake datetime class whose strftime('%s') returns the given\n    values in sequence (simulating platforms where %s is unsupported).\"\"\"\n    results = list(epoch_strftime_results)\n\n    class FakeDateTime:\n        def __init__(self, ts, utc=False):\n            self._ts = ts\n            self._utc = utc\n\n        @classmethod\n        def fromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n            return cls(ts)\n\n        @classmethod\n        def utcfromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n            return cls(ts, utc=True)\n\n        def strftime(self, fmt):\n            if fmt == '%s':\n                if results:\n                    return results.pop(0)\n                return str(int(self._ts))\n            if self._utc:\n                return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(self._ts, datetime.timezone.utc).strftime(fmt)\n            return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(self._ts).strftime(fmt)\n\n    return FakeDateTime\n```\n\nWait, `str(int(self._ts))` default \u2014 for test 1, pass results=['1500000000'] or just let default handle: pass empty list \u2192 default str(int(ts)) = '1500000000'. Good.\n\nNote: `now` and `utcnow` are separate instances; the '%s' calls only happen on `now` (lines 49, 54). utcnow's strftime is called only for iso8601 formats. So the results queue is consumed only by `now`. Good.\n\nHmm, one subtlety: `results.pop(0)` \u2014 mutable closure state; fresh list per factory call. Fine.\n\nNow the fake time module. Also note: the real code calls `time.strftime(\"%Z\")` and `time.strftime(\"%z\")` and `time.tzname[1]`. My fake time namespace:\n\n```python\n_FAKE_TIME = types.SimpleNamespace(\n    time=lambda: 1500000000.123456,\n    strftime=lambda fmt: {'%Z': 'CET', '%z': '+0100'}[fmt],\n    tzname=('CET', 'CEST'),\n)\n```\n\nBut wait \u2014 should each test get a fresh fake time? It's stateless (lambda, dict). A module-level constant is fine; monkeypatch.setattr restores the original after each test. But defining it at module level and patching the module attr \u2014 no pollution. OK.\n\nHmm, actually, should I patch `time` in the module under test, or patch the global `time.time`? Patching the module's reference is cleaner (no global side effects even transiently). Do it.\n\nNow, the module import. The test file:\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nimport types\n\nimport pytest\n\nimport ansible.module_utils.facts.system.date_time as date_time_module\nfrom ansible.module_utils.facts.system.date_time import DateTimeFactCollector\n\n_REAL_DATETIME = datetime.datetime\n_EPOCH_TS = 1500000000.123456\n```\n\nWait \u2014 is the module path right? `ansible/module_utils/facts/system/date_time.py` \u2192 `ansible.module_utils.facts.system.date_time`. Yes.\n\nHelper to install fakes:\n\n```python\ndef _install_fakes(monkeypatch, epoch_strftime_results):\n    fake_datetime_cls = _make_fake_datetime_class(epoch_strftime_results)\n    fake_datetime_module = types.SimpleNamespace(datetime=fake_datetime_cls)\n    fake_time_module = types.SimpleNamespace(\n        time=lambda: _EPOCH_TS,\n        strftime=lambda fmt: {'%Z': 'CET', '%z': '+0100'}[fmt],\n        tzname=('CET', 'CEST'),\n    )\n    monkeypatch.setattr(date_time_module, 'datetime', fake_datetime_module)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(date_time_module, 'time', fake_time_module)\n    return fake_datetime_cls\n```\n\nAnd a helper to build expected values:\n\n```python\ndef _expected_now():\n    return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(_EPOCH_TS)\n\ndef _expected_utcnow():\n    return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(_EPOCH_TS, datetime.timezone.utc)\n```\n\nHmm, `datetime.timezone.utc` \u2014 fine.\n\nTest 1:\n\n```python\ndef test_collect_builds_full_date_time_facts(monkeypatch):\n    _install_fakes(monkeypatch, [])\n    facts = DateTimeFactCollector().collect()\n    assert set(facts.keys()