tc)` \u2014 no deprecation. And `_expected_utcnow()` uses `fromtimestamp(ts, timezone.utc)`. Good, no warnings.\n\nWait, one thing: in `_expected_utcnow`, `fromtimestamp(ts, tz=utc)` returns an AWARE datetime. `aware.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%fZ')` \u2014 works, gives UTC wall time. And the fake's utc strftime does the same. Equal. Good.\n\nAlso `%W` \u2014 weeknumber: `now.strftime('%W')` \u2014 platform-dependent? %W is standard C; glibc supports. On Windows, %W is supported by time.strftime? Python's time.strftime on Windows supports %W? MSVCRT doesn't support %W natively, but Python's time.strftime implements some directives itself? Actually Python's time.strftime on Windows raises ValueError for unsupported ones? Hmm, datetime.strftime via _wrap_strftime \u2192 time.strftime. On Windows, time.strftime is implemented in C (timemodule.c) which calls the system strftime but also pre-handles some? I believe Python's time.strftime passes most to system strftime; on Windows %W might produce empty or wrong. But since both the fake delegation and the expected computation use the SAME platform strftime, the comparison holds regardless. And the test env is presumably Linux anyway. Fine.\n\nOne more: `all(isinstance(v, str) for v in dt.values())` \u2014 all values are str. Yes.\n\nAlso assert `facts_dict` structure: `facts == {'date_time': dt}` \u2014 set(keys) check suffices.\n\nNow, cleanup: monkeypatch handles restoration. The fake classes are local. No global state modified besides monkeypatched module attrs. The collector instance is stateless. Good. No files, no env vars.\n\nAlso \u2014 \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 module-level: imports, constants, helper functions, test functions. No pytest.main. Good.\n\nLet me also double-check: does `DateTimeFactCollector` require anything from BaseFactCollector on instantiation? BaseFactCollector.__init__ takes namespace=None presumably. `DateTimeFactCollector()` \u2014 BaseFactCollector has `def __init__(self, namespace=None): self.namespace = namespace` or similar. Fine.\n\nDoes `collect` use `self._fact_ids` or anything? No, the shown collect doesn't. Good.\n\nLet me also verify the get_info tool \u2014 the task says \"Use the get_info tool function as necessary.\" I could use it to check `DateTimeFactCollector.collect` or `BaseFactCollector`. It might help confirm the module structure. Let me call get_info on 'DateTimeFactCollector.collect' and maybe 'BaseFactCollector' to verify signatures. Since the prompt already gives the source, it's arguably unnecessary, but the instructions say to use it as necessary. I'll make a couple of independent calls in one block: get_info('DateTimeFactCollector.collect') and get_info('BaseFactCollector'). These are independent \u2192 same block.\n\nThen write the final test code.\n\nLet me now write the complete test file.\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nimport types\n\nimport pytest\n\nimport ansible.module_utils.facts.system.date_time as date_time_module\nfrom ansible.module_utils.facts.system.date_time import DateTimeFactCollector\n\n# Real datetime class, captured before any patching.\n_REAL_DATETIME = datetime.datetime\n\n# Fixed timestamp used by the fake time module: 2017-07-14 02:40:00.123456 UTC.\n_EPOCH_TS = 1500000000.123456\n\n_ALL_DATE_TIME_KEYS = {\n    'year', 'month', 'weekday', 'weekday_number', 'weeknumber', 'day',\n    'hour', 'minute', 'second', 'epoch', 'epoch_int', 'date', 'time',\n    'iso8601_micro', 'iso8601', 'iso8601_basic', 'iso8601_basic_short',\n    'tz', 'tz_dst', 'tz_offset',\n}\n\n\ndef _make_fake_datetime_class(epoch_strftime_results):\n    \"\"\"Return a stand-in for datetime.datetime.\n\n    ``strftime('%s')`` returns the given values in order (used to simulate\n    platforms where the %s directive is unsupported); once the list is\n    exhausted it returns ``str(int(ts))``.  All other formats are delegated\n    to the real datetime implementation for the very same timestamp, so the\n    expected values can be computed independently in the tests.\n    \"\"\"\n    epoch_results = list(epoch_strftime_results)\n\n    class FakeDateTime:\n        def __init__(self, ts, utc=False):\n            self._ts = ts\n            self._utc = utc\n\n        @classmethod\n        def fromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n            return cls(ts)\n\n        @classmethod\n        def utcfromtimestamp(cls, ts):\n            return cls(ts, utc=True)\n\n        def strftime(self, fmt):\n            if fmt == '%s':\n                if epoch_results:\n                    return epoch_results.pop(0)\n                return str(int(self._ts))\n            if self._utc:\n                return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(\n                    self._ts, datetime.timezone.utc).strftime(fmt)\n            return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(self._ts).strftime(fmt)\n\n    return FakeDateTime\n\n\ndef _install_fakes(monkeypatch, epoch_strftime_results):\n    \"\"\"Patch the module under test to use deterministic datetime/time fakes.\"\"\"\n    fake_datetime_cls = _make_fake_datetime_class(epoch_strftime_results)\n    fake_datetime_module = types.SimpleNamespace(datetime=fake_datetime_cls)\n    fake_time_module = types.SimpleNamespace(\n        time=lambda: _EPOCH_TS,\n        strftime=lambda fmt: {'%Z': 'CET', '%z': '+0100'}[fmt],\n        tzname=('CET', 'CEST'),\n    )\n    monkeypatch.setattr(date_time_module, 'datetime', fake_datetime_module)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(date_time_module, 'time', fake_time_module)\n    return fake_datetime_cls\n\n\ndef _local_now():\n    return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(_EPOCH_TS)\n\n\ndef _utc_now():\n    return _REAL_DATETIME.fromtimestamp(_EPOCH_TS, datetime.timezone.utc)\n\n\ndef test_collect_returns_all_date_time_facts(monkeypatch):\n    _install_fakes(monkeypatch, [])\n\n    facts = DateTimeFactCollector().collect()\n\n    assert set(facts) == {'date_time'}\n    dt = facts['date_time']\n    assert set(dt) == _ALL_DATE_TIME_KEYS\n    assert all(isinstance(value, str) for value in dt.values())\n\n    now = _local_now()\n    utcnow = _utc_now()\n    assert dt['year'] == now.strftime('%Y')\n    assert dt['month'] == now.strftime('%m')\n    assert dt['weekday'] == now.strftime('%A')\n    assert dt['weekday_number'] == now.strftime('%w')\n    assert dt['weeknumber'] == now.strftime('%W')\n    assert dt['day'] == now.strftime('%d')\n    assert dt['hour'] == now.strftime('%H')\n    assert dt['minute'] == now.strftime('%M')\n    assert dt['second'] == now.strftime('%S')\n    # %s is supported by the fake, so no fallback happens for epoch/epoch_int.\n    assert dt['epoch'] == str(int(_EPOCH_TS))\n    assert dt['epoch_int'] == str(int(_EPOCH_TS))\n    assert dt['date'] == now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n    assert dt['time'] == now.strftime('%H:%M:%S')\n    assert dt['iso8601_micro'] == utcnow.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%fZ')\n    assert dt['iso8601'] == utcnow.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')\n    assert dt['iso8601_basic'] == now.strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%S%f')\n    assert dt['iso8601_basic_short'] == now.strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%S')\n    assert dt['tz'] == 'CET'\n    assert dt['tz_dst'] == 'CEST'\n    assert dt['tz_offset'] == '+0100'\n\n\ndef test_collect_epoch_falls_back_when_strftime_returns_empty_string(monkeypatch):\n    # First %s lookup (the 'epoch' fact) returns '' as on platforms where the\n    # directive is unsupported; the second lookup returns a valid value so the\n    # epoch_int conversion does not raise and the collector can finish.\n    _install_fakes(monkeypatch, ['', str(int(_EPOCH_TS))])\n\n    facts = DateTimeFactCollector().collect()\n\n    dt = facts['date_time']\n    assert dt['epoch'] == str(int(_EPOCH_TS))\n    assert dt['epoch_int'] == str(int(_EPOCH_TS))\n    assert set(dt) == _ALL_DATE_TIME_KEYS\n    assert dt['date'] == _local_now().strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n    assert dt['time'] == _local_now().strftime('%H:%M:%S')\n\n\ndef test_collect_epoch_falls_back_when_strftime_echoes_directive(monkeypatch):\n    # Fir